\chapter{Backend, target, and optimization selection}
\label{ch:65}
\index{Backend, target, and optimization selection}

Project selection can choose \OnxCodeTerm{c11}, \OnxCodeTerm{mir-c11}, \OnxCodeTerm{mir-vm}, or \OnxCodeTerm{mir-archon} in the documented profiles. A scoped or CLI override changes the effective plan; the driver rejects an unavailable path instead of falling back silently. A VM build produces an O1VM artifact, while the native Archon path needs matching target tools and ABI archives. The optimization profile and pass overrides are separate from artifact kind.

\section{Inspect before compiling}
Use \OnxCodeTerm{on1x-project.py backends} with the intended compiler to probe configured paths. Use the project plan with a backend override to inspect the tools, flags and output kind. For differential checks start with a scalar function that both backends admit. Add an ownership feature in a second fixture to make the representation boundary visible. When reporting different optimization results, include both effective profiles. Named MIR passes cannot simply be applied to an artifact generated directly from HIR.

\section{Resolve a release build in three decisions}
Consider this fragment of a root manifest:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
backend c11
backend "release" mir-vm
optimization-profile debug
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
First resolve the build profile. With \OnxCodeTerm{on1xc exec release},
the directory is the release generation. Next resolve the backend: the
release-specific entry overrides the project-wide C11 selection. Finally
resolve compiler optimization: the explicit manifest preset takes precedence
over the implicit preset associated with a release build. Thus the plan
describes a VM bytecode build with debug compiler policy. It does not pass
\OnxCodeTerm{-O3} to a host C compiler; this backend has no such step.

Now append \OnxCodeTerm{--backend mir-c11} to that command. The CLI
changes the second decision only. For an admitted scalar program and a
working native toolchain the resulting artifact is a host executable.
The ordinary release C flags now apply to the generated C, even though
the On1x compiler still uses the debug optimization preset. To change the
latter decision as well, explicitly select \OnxCodeTerm{--opt-profile release}.

A useful check is to predict what must be copied to run each build on
another compatible host. The native executable and the VM launcher's
executable bit alone tell little about their contents. The VM generation
needs the bytecode and its bundled hidden runner beside the launcher.
Compare artifact names and hashes in the build report before treating a
missing runner as a failed compiler optimization. A CLI backend override
belongs in the build report; it does not rewrite the locked manifest policy.

